WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.827
Duplicates can inflate counts, totals, and averages, but not every repeated value is a duplicate.

00:00:07.827 --> 00:00:12.602
You first need to decide what defines a unique observation.

00:00:12.602 --> 00:00:17.877
In this dataset, order ID is supposed to identify one order.

00:00:17.877 --> 00:00:24.507
If the same order ID appears twice with the same information, that may be a true duplicate.

00:00:24.507 --> 00:00:31.444
But a customer ID could legitimately appear many times because one customer can place many orders.

00:00:31.926 --> 00:00:37.503
Run the code and inspect the rows that share the same order ID before removing anything.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:41.318
This is safer than calling drop duplicates immediately.

00:00:41.318 --> 00:00:48.018
The code uses the subset argument to focus on the column that should be unique.

00:00:48.018 --> 00:00:54.358
In a real project, you may need several columns together to define uniqueness, such as patient ID plus visit date,

00:00:54.358 --> 00:00:57.538
or product ID plus transaction ID.

00:00:57.538 --> 00:01:03.759
The key idea is to understand the meaning of the identifier before deciding what is duplicated.
